ctually web_crawl is the sanctioned tool for fetching web pages. But it says \"same-origin web site\" crawl. Let me try both: run_command curl for specific URLs (if network allowed), and web_crawl as backup.\n\n5. Formalize: precise statement of the Collatz map T(n) = n/2 if even, (3n+1)/2 if odd (accelerated) or 3n+1 if odd (standard). State the conjecture: for all n ≥ 1, exists k with T^k(n) = 1. Also state the computational claim: ∀ 1 ≤ n ≤ 10^7, trajectory reaches 1 (verified). Provide a Lean 4 theorem statement sketch (not compiled unless Lean available — check if lean is installed; probably not; be honest). Maybe provide Isabelle/Lean statement as code block with disclaimer \"not machine-checked here\".\n\n6. Counterexample-hunter: beyond verification, note what a counterexample would look like (divergent orbit or nontrivial cycle), and that our verifier would catch divergence only within the range... Actually a divergent orbit starting at n ≤ 10^7 would be caught since we iterate until below n... wait, if the orbit diverges, it never goes below n? Not true — it could dip below n at some point then diverge later from a smaller value... but smaller values are also verified, so by induction if all n ≤ 10^7 reach a value below themselves... hmm, the induction: if every n ≤ N reaches some value < n (which then by strong induction reaches 1), then all n ≤ N reach 1. A divergent orbit from n ≤ 10^7 would have to never dip below n... but wait, it could dip below n and then the smaller starting value diverges — but that smaller value is also ≤ 10^7 and verified by the same induction. The induction is: assume all m < n reach 1; show n reaches 1. If n's orbit ever hits m < n, done. If it never does, we iterate... forever? No — we iterate until we hit a value < n; if that never happens the program would loop forever. The rigorous statement: the program terminates for each n iff the orbit of n reaches a value < n. For n ≤ 10^7, the program terminated (we observed it), so 