++{"seq": 71, "ts": 1792083679.448, "role": "assistant", "content": "The crawl artifact stores truncated HTML (header chrome only — article body cut off). Switching to Wikipedia's REST API (same origin) for plain-text extracts:", "reasoning_content": "The body_text is truncated HTML (only the header chrome, cut off before article content — the crawl tool stored only the first ~30KB of each page's HTML). The article body with the actual claims (4×10^18, Helfgott, Oliveira e Silva) is NOT in the stored body_text — it got truncated at the header/nav. So the crawl artifact doesn't contain the substantive text I need.\n\nBetter approach: use the Wikipedia REST API which returns plain text extracts. But web_crawl is the only network tool... it crawls same-origin pages. Can I point web_crawl at the REST API URL? It's same-origin (en.wikipedia.org). Let me try:\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/api/rest_v1/page/summary/Goldbach%27s_conjecture — short summary\n- The full extract: https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&titles=Goldbach%27s_conjecture&format=json\n\nThe body_text field seems to store raw HTML of the page (truncated). The REST API returns JSON with plain text \"extract\" — much easier to parse. Let me try web_crawl on the api.php URL with max_pages=1.\n\nAlternatively, delegate the extraction to a sub-agent as the mentor suggested — the sub-agent can read the artifact files and grep. But the artifact itself lacks the article text (truncated at nav). Actually wait — is it truncated? The body_text for the Goldbach page ended at \"Personal tools</sp\" — yes truncated mid-tag. The crawl tool stores a truncated preview. So the article content is not available in the artifact.\n\nHmm, but wait — maybe the crawl.json contains more of the page elsewhere... The body_text field is one long JSON string; my sed-based extraction produced 2588 lines but grep found no substantive content. The page HTML stored is truncated (~30KB of ~200KB). The article body comes after the header chrome in Wikipedia's HTML, so it's cut off.\n\nPlan: use web_crawl directly on the Wikipedia REST API endpoints (same origin):\n1. https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&exsectionformat=plain&titles=Goldbach%27s_conjecture&format=json — full plain-text extract\n2. Same for Goldbach's weak conjecture\n3. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_Oliveira_e_Silva — his article (may contain the 4×10^18 bound)\n4. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harald_Helfgott — ternary proof info\n\nAlso OEIS A006880 for π(10^6) cross-check: https://oeis.org/A006880 — allowed domain.\n\nLet me try the API URL first with max_pages=1. If web_crawl refuses non-HTML or the query string, I'll see.", "tool_calls": [{"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a91fdbbfbf9b4b5b", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "web_crawl", "arguments": "{\"delay\": 1, \"max_pages\": 1, \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&exsectionformat=plain&titles=Goldbach%27s_conjecture&format=json&formatversion=2\"}"}}], "step": 30, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 722, "prompt_tokens": 69549, "total_tokens": 70271, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 615}}}
++{"seq": 72, "ts": 1792083693.656, "role": "tool", "content": "{\"pages_crawled\": 1, \"endpoints\": [\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&exsectionformat=plain&titles=Goldbach%27s_conjecture&format=json&formatversion=2\"], \"common_paths\": {\"/robots.txt\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 579}, \"sitemap.xml\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 581}, \"admin/\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 571}, \"login\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 568}, \"api/\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 567}, \".git/HEAD\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 577}, \"backup/\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 573}, \"wp-admin/\": {\"status\": 200, \"bytes\": 577}}, \"cookies\": {\"WMF-Last-Access\": \"15-Oct-2026\", \"WMF-Last-Access-Global\": \"15-Oct-2026\", \"GeoIP\": \"IN:UP:Noida:28.58:77.33:v4\", \"NetworkProbeLimit\": \"0.001\", \"WMF-Uniq\": \"89DGb7K8ZmtrouMz1w6RmAP6AAEBAFvdVjRREkO7mIAiVtq_oKJdFzEkmcJQawDm\"}, \"artifact_ref\": \"artifacts/3326642cb17351f8/crawl.json\", \"first_page_text\": \"{\\\"batchcomplete\\\":true,\\\"query\\\":{\\\"normalized\\\":[{\\\"fromencoded\\\":false,\\\"from\\\":\\\"Goldbach's_conjecture\\\",\\\"to\\\":\\\"Goldbach's conjecture\\\"}],\\\"pages\\\":[{\\\"pageid\\\":44758,\\\"ns\\\":0,\\\"title\\\":\\\"Goldbach's conjecture\\\",\\\"extract\\\":\\\"Goldbach's conjecture is one of the oldest and best-known unsolved problems in number theory and all of mathematics. It states that every even natural number greater than 2 is the sum of two prime numbers.\\\\nThe conjecture has been shown to hold for all natural numbers less than 4×1018, but remains unproven despite considerable effort.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nHistory\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nOrigins\\\\nOn 7 June 1742, the Prussian mathematician Christian Goldbach wrote a letter to Leonhard Euler (letter XLIII), in which he proposed the following conjecture:\\\\n\\\\nEvery integer that can be written as the sum of two primes can also be written as the sum of as many primes (including unity) as one wishes, until all terms are units.\\\\nGoldbach was following the now-abandoned convention of considering 1 to be a prime number, so that a sum of units would be a sum of primes. He then proposed a second conjecture in the margin of his letter, which implies the first:\\\\n\\\\nIt seems at least, that every integer greater than 2 can be written as the sum of three primes.\\\\nEuler replied in a letter dated 30 June 1742 and reminded Goldbach of an earlier conversation they had had (\\\\\\\"... so Ew. vormals mit mir communicirt haben ...\\\\\\\"), in which Goldbach had remarked that the first of those two conjectures would follow from the statement\\\\n\\\\nThis is in fact equivalent to his second, marginal conjecture. In the letter dated 30 June 1742, Euler stated:\\\\n\\\\nThat ... every even integer is a sum of two primes, I regard as a completely certain theorem, although I cannot prove it.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nWeaker Descartes' conjecture\\\\nRené Descartes wrote, \\\\\\\"Every even number can be expressed as the sum of at most three primes.\\\\\\\" This proposition is similar to, but weaker than, Goldbach's conjecture. Paul Erdős said, \\\\\\\"Descartes actually discovered this before Goldbach ... but it is better that the conjecture was named for Goldbach because, mathematically speaking, Descartes was infinitely rich and Goldbach was very poor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nPartial results\\\\nGoldbach's conjecture involving the sum of two primes is much more difficult than the weak Goldbach conjecture, which says that every odd integer greater than 5 is the sum of three primes. Using Vinogradov's method, Nikolai Chudakov, Johannes van der Corput, and Theodor Estermann showed (1937–1938) that almost all even numbers can be written as the sum of two primes (in the sense that the fraction of even numbers up to some N which can be so written tends towards 1 as N increases). In 1930, Lev Schnirelmann proved that any natural number greater than 1 can be written as the sum of not more than C prime numbers, where C is an effectively computable constant; see Schnirelmann density. Schnirelmann's constant is the lowest number C with this property. Schnirelmann himself obtained C < 800000. This result was subsequently enhanced by many authors, such as Olivier Ramaré, who in 1995 showed that every even number n ≥ 4 is in fact the sum of at most 6 primes. The best known result stems from the proof of the weak Goldbach conjecture by Harald Helfgott, which directly implies that every even number n ≥ 4 is the sum of at most 4 primes.\\\\nIn 1924, Hardy and Littlewood showed under the assumption of the generalized Riemann hypothesis that the number of even numbers up to X violating the Goldbach conjecture is much less than X1⁄2 + c for small c.\\\\nIn 1948, using sieve theory methods, Alfréd Rényi showed that every sufficiently large even number can be written as the sum of a prime and an almost prime with at most K factors. Chen Jingrun showed in 1973 using sieve theory that every sufficiently large even number can be written as the sum of either two primes, or a prime and a semiprime (the product of two primes). See Chen's theorem for further information.\\\\nIn 1975, Hugh Lowell Montgomery and \"}", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a91fdbbfbf9b4b5b", "name": "web_crawl", "step": 30}
